WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
ABI stability becomes manageable when it is measured.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:16.760
ABI baselines, exported-symbol checks, binary compatibility tools, header review, dependency lockfiles, release checklist items, and CI gates can help detect unintended changes.

3
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:25.180
These controls are most useful when the team has already identified which interfaces are promised to be stable and which are internal implementation details.

4
00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:39.940
Tests should include old clients with new libraries where practical, plugin compatibility checks, version negotiation, allocation and release paths, error translation, initialization, and shutdown behavior.

5
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:47.160
Build matrix testing can also catch compiler, runtime, and configuration differences that source-only tests miss.

6
00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:53.100
Verification should inspect the artifacts that will actually ship, not only the headers that developers read.

7
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.380
Governance matters because a binary compatibility promise is a product commitment.

8
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:07.720
Documentation should state supported platforms, supported build modes, stable interfaces, internal interfaces, deprecation policy, and migration expectations.

9
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:16.940
Release review should ask whether ABI changes were intentional, whether compatibility impact is documented, and whether downstream users have a safe upgrade path.

10
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.800
Defects found by ABI tooling should become tracked engineering work.

11
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.260
Ownership should be assigned so compatibility decisions do not drift between teams.

12
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.780
The governance model should fit the size of the product, but it must be explicit.

13
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.200
A small library might need a short release checklist and symbol comparison.

14
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:42.660
A large platform may need compatibility councils, baseline artifacts, staged deprecations, and partner validation.

15
00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:49.320
In both cases, the objective is the same: make binary compatibility a managed engineering decision.
